WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:05.680
Casey Frey's "Get tf out my way type way" is one&nbsp;
of the most artfully produced videos online today&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:10.000
the video tells the story of a man rejecting&nbsp;
criticism and using dance to express his true&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:15.040
self but within such a seemingly simple story&nbsp;
Frey uses innovative film language and symbolism&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.040 --> 00:00:19.760
to create not only an addictive to watch viral&nbsp;
video but also a cinematic masterpiece that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.760 --> 00:00:25.040
continues to impact the dance videos we see&nbsp;
today first we see the main character chop&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.040 --> 00:00:29.760
a piece of wood then the camera pans up to a&nbsp;
medium shot notice how the character straightens&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:34.160
up to full height as the camera follows him&nbsp;
this movement with the camera adds energy to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.160 --> 00:00:38.320
the shot and guides our eyesight to his first&nbsp;
shoulder dance move near the top of the frame

00:00:41.680 --> 00:00:46.080
this same camera movement later visualizes&nbsp;
his connection with his good alter ego&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:50.480
after the good alter ego pulses his chest&nbsp;
we cut to the main character and both he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.480 --> 00:00:53.760
and the camera are looking inward&nbsp;
but then they both move outward&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:58.400
showing how the good alter ego's contagious&nbsp;
energy infected him on the inside from here&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.400 --> 00:01:03.760
the camera moves with full force it travels up&nbsp;
his body as his energy moves from his foot up to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.760 --> 00:01:11.840
his hips then to his arms and his good alter ego&nbsp;
similarly moves with the camera as they connect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.960 --> 00:01:17.760
Frey uses matching shots to seamlessly transition&nbsp;
between the two characters as they move as one and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:22.880
they finally join forces in the final frame of&nbsp;
the video to add contrast to this connection the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.880 --> 00:01:28.400
bad alter ego is filmed in a mostly static frame&nbsp;
and the camera doesn't follow his movements at all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:32.640
in his last shot he rejects the movement&nbsp;
entirely frank gives his video energy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:36.400
not just from the camera but from his cuts&nbsp;
like when he cuts to the beat of the music

00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:43.600
he also skips frames to make his movement appear&nbsp;
more snappy or when he flips off his bad alter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:49.200
ego repeats frames to add emphasis to his leg and&nbsp;
hand movements these cuts combined with the camera&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.200 --> 00:01:53.280
movement create a sequence with an electric&nbsp;
hypnotizing energy to really get a feel for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.280 --> 00:01:57.760
how well he executed this video compare it to&nbsp;
the countless recreation videos while many of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.760 --> 00:02:02.320
them are great they still reveal just how much&nbsp;
precision Frey put into the editing and camera&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.320 --> 00:02:07.360
work in the original but "Get tf out of my way&nbsp;
type way" is more than just a snappy dance video&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.360 --> 00:02:12.080
it has its own story one with a complete Hero's&nbsp;
Journey the main character has a call to adventure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.080 --> 00:02:17.200
when starting his dance but he refuses the call&nbsp;
after getting rejected then he meets a mentor who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.200 --> 00:02:22.320
helps him cross the threshold overcome the abyss&nbsp;
of rejection and transform the story's meaning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.320 --> 00:02:26.560
can be interpreted in many ways and I'm not the&nbsp;
first to acknowledge it feels like it's about the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.560 --> 00:02:31.920
worker's revolution it can be read as the story of&nbsp;
a working class man rejecting his manual labor job&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.920 --> 00:02:36.000
and joining a collective in that case the&nbsp;
heartbeat is a symbol of the awakening of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:40.400
class consciousness in the worker and the bad&nbsp;
alter ego is a symbol for the ruling class who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.400 --> 00:02:44.640
wants to maintain the status quo there's even&nbsp;
a prominent sign on the glass door that slides&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:49.680
across the frame that says "this property is under&nbsp;
video surveillance" a message which makes the area&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.680 --> 00:02:54.560
feel like an intrusive work zone but i think a&nbsp;
big reason Frey's video has remained so relevant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.560 --> 00:02:59.680
for so long is because of its universal message of&nbsp;
rejecting the haters and embracing your true self&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.680 --> 00:03:04.240
that general appeal may partly explain&nbsp;
why the video became its own meme format&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.240 --> 00:03:08.560
in this meme the bad alter ego can be any&nbsp;
negative force and the main character is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.560 --> 00:03:13.280
rejection of that instruction and phrase video&nbsp;
continues to impact the dance videos we see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.280 --> 00:03:17.680
online it's one of the most popular examples&nbsp;
of having the camera follow the movement of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.680 --> 00:03:22.320
the dancer a technique that has become a dance&nbsp;
video staple and the storytelling in his dance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.320 --> 00:03:26.720
moves sets a standard for character-driven&nbsp;
choreography and for anybody who doesn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.720 --> 00:03:31.840
appreciate the cinematic artistry of this video&nbsp;
all i have to say to you is get out my way

